.he "crypt (1)                    9/30/26                    crypt (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
crypt
- encode and decode a file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
crypt [ key ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Crypt reads its standard input and writes its standard output,
enciphering as it goes according to the key.
.sp
Crypt is its own inverse; to read an enciphered file back, all you
need is the same key word.
.sp
The key is a word, and only its first eight characters are used.
Capital letters are not the same as small ones, so bandersnatch and
Bandersnatch are two different keys and neither will read the other's
file.
.sp
If no key is named, crypt asks for one - but only when its standard
input is a terminal.  With the input coming from a file there is no
terminal to type on, and the only thing left to read a key from is
the file being enciphered, so crypt complains and exits instead.
.sp
Note: this is not a very sophisticated encryption scheme (NSA would
have it overnight).  You can increase the complexity of the
encryption by using multiple passes of crypt, each with a different
key word.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
.in +5
.br
.bd
crypt bandersnatch < file > encrypted
.sp
Encipher a file.
.sp
.bd
crypt bandersnatch < encrypted
.sp
Read the enciphered file back.
.sp
.bd
crypt honeybee < file | crypt Beaumont | crypt oscar > encrypted
.sp
Encipher a file with three key words, one pass each.  All three are
needed, in the same order, to read it back.

.in -5
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/usr/lib/makekey - turns the key word into the characters crypt
enciphers with
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is not the
.bd
crypt
of
.bd
crypt (3).
That one hashes a password for /etc/passwd; this one enciphers a
file.  They are two different routines that happen to share a name,
and neither does the other's job: the hash in /etc/passwd is not a
thing this command can be given, and login cannot read a file this
one wrote.
.sp
The key word is not used as it stands.  Crypt forks
.bd
makekey (1),
which folds the key and two characters of salt into thirteen
characters, and those are what the three tables are built from -
which is why the key is not recoverable from the enciphered file
without trying keys one at a time.
.sp
The 1982 micronix /bin/crypt is a different cipher.  One byte of its
output depends only on the leading characters of the key it has read
so far, where every byte of this one's depends on the whole key
through makekey, so the two disagree on every file and neither reads
what the other wrote.  What is described above is this command, which
is the seventh edition's crypt.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
crypt (3), makekey (1), getpass (3), login (1)
